        "subtitle": "A day trip to Antarctica",
        "text": "The pilot's calm voice came over the loudspeakers as our Qantas 747 taxied down the runway at Sydney Airport in Australia: 'We'll be taking off in a southerly direction. Watch check: 8:20 a.m.' Our day trip had begun. We were going to Antarctica. For the first four hours, most of which was a straight line across the ocean, we could have been anywhere. What makes the outward journey memorable, though, is the chance to learn about the science and history of the seventh continent from people who have lived and worked there.\n\nThere were four specialists on this particular trip: Pat Quilty, a geologist and former head of the Australian Antarctic programme; Polar Medal winner Syd Kirkby, who has surveyed more Antarctic territory than any other polar explorer; Di Patterson, the first woman to be put in charge of an Antarctic research station; and Warren Papworth, who knows more about emperor penguins than the penguins do themselves. Instead of in-flight films, we were entertained, amazed and painlessly educated about Antarctica.\n\nUntil about 160 million years ago, Antarctica was part of the ancient continent of Gondwanaland, before Gondwanaland broke up into the continents we know today. A few minutes before noon, the pilot, Captain Dennis, told us all to take out the compasses we had been asked to bring. As I put mine on my knee, the needle moved away from north-south and began to spin wildly. We were flying straight over the South Magnetic Pole which, unlike the geographic pole, moves about 6km a year.\n\nAs I looked out of my window at the Southern Ocean below, the outermost ice floes could be seen directly under the wing, looking like the pieces of a huge natural jigsaw. Then came Dumont d'Urville, a base on Antarctica. We carried on along the coast before turning south over the Ross Ice Shelf.\n\nBy the end of these middle four hours of the journey, we had seen ice cliffs, powder ice, amazing blue ice (even the scientists don't know what makes it blue), many kinds of glacier and, last but not least, magnificent rivers of ice sliding into the sea. All this grandeur largely made up for any disappointment at not catching sight of the penguins Antarctica is famous for, let alone a colony. The reason was a good one: flying low enough to see the penguins clearly would seriously disturb them.\n\nWe passed the Trans-Antarctic Mountains, which Captain Dennis called 'one of the loveliest mountain ranges on the surface of the Earth'. But for me, the highlight of this unforgettable day trip was Mount Minto, which rises more than 4,000m above the Ross Ice Shelf.\n\nAll too soon, it was time to turn in a half-circle and begin the trip back to Sydney and civilisation. This is one trip that can certainly be recommended to people who want to do something a little different. From November to February, the 'season' for visiting Antarctica, the weather is remarkably consistent. The air temperature outside is around freezing, but it is very sunny. Because you won't be landing anywhere, the journey counts as a domestic flight, so, sadly, you won't get an Antarctic stamp in your passport, which you don't need to bring on this trip. But, by way of compensation, you will receive a commemorative certificate stating that you have crossed the Antarctic continent, 'thereby joining the exclusive group of travellers who have achieved this feat'."
